---
description: Every figure shown on the site must be computed from real data, never invented
alwaysApply: true
---

# Real Numbers Only

The owner has stated this as a hard constraint: no illustrative, projected, or
placeholder figures anywhere on the site. A plausible-looking invented number is
the worst possible outcome, because it will get repeated in a sales conversation.

## Compute, do not hardcode

Derive every figure from the corpus at build time via `lib/` helpers such as
`getDivisionRollup`, `getVisibilitySnapshot`, and `getReportingGaps`. Never type a
count into JSX.

## Say zero out loud

When a metric is zero because the data is not captured yet, print the zero and
explain why in the surrounding copy. Do not hide the row or substitute a sample.
Bid winners and installed products are all zero today, and the reporting page says
so plainly.

## Never let prose contradict a computed value

Copy that describes a number must be conditional on it, because the number moves
when the corpus changes.

```tsx
// BAD: hardcoded claim that silently becomes false
<p>It is zero. No source names CRH.</p>

// GOOD: derived from the same value the reader sees
<p>{crhNamed === 0
  ? `Across all ${total} indexed projects, not one source names CRH`
  : `Across ${total} indexed projects, only ${crhNamed} name CRH`}</p>
```

## Verify before shipping

After a build, re-derive the numbers independently from the shipped source and
compare against the rendered HTML in `out/`. Ordering and matching logic can shift
counts in ways that are not obvious. A discrepancy is either a real bug or a
difference in method that must be understood before deploying, not waved through.

## Word boundaries when matching brands

Use `termAppearsIn` from `lib/format.ts`, never `String.includes`. Substring
matching produced false positives such as "RDI" hitting inside "Nordic", which
inflated brand mention counts.
